**Lemma 9.2 (credited one-ray mechanism, including complex multiplicity).**
Each actual low live-bearing label in the sharp weak-cost class has
dimension two and contributes one projective real-null coefficient ray,
even with arbitrary inaccessible complex encoder and decoder Kraus
multiplicity. This is the RH8.2 mechanism.

Proof. On the supported qutrit write its encoder matrices as
$C_j:\mathbb C^3\to\mathbb C^d$ and compressed live-decoder matrices
as $R_i:\mathbb C^d\to\mathbb C^3$, all for this ONE actual label.
Proposition 2.2 makes every nonzero $R_iC_j$ a phase multiple of

$$
K(x_0,z_0,w_0)=\begin{pmatrix}
x_0h_0&z_0a_K&w_0c_K\\z_0b_K&x_0h_1&0\\w_0d_K&0&x_0h_2
\end{pmatrix},\qquad x_0\ne0,
\tag{EP.60}
$$

with real projective ratios and rank exactly two. Thus $d=1$ cannot
be live-bearing. For $d=2$, fix a nonzero $R_{i_0}C_{j_0}$. Rank two
makes $R_{i_0}$ injective and $C_{j_0}$ onto. Every other nonzero
supported $C_j$ gives a nonzero rank-two product $R_{i_0}C_j$ with
image $\operatorname{ran}R_{i_0}$.

The left null line of EP.60 is generated, after removal of the common
phase, by

$$
\left(1,-\frac{a_Kz_0}{h_1x_0},-\frac{c_Kw_0}{h_2x_0}\right)^T.
\tag{EP.61}
$$

Since $a_K,c_K,h_1,h_2$ are nonzero, the image determines $z_0/x_0$
and $w_0/x_0$. Therefore all $R_{i_0}C_j$ are proportional to
$R_{i_0}C_{j_0}$, and injectivity makes all supported $C_j$
proportional to $C_{j_0}$. For every other nonzero $R_i$,
surjectivity of $C_{j_0}$ makes $R_iC_{j_0}$ nonzero and rank two.
Its right kernel is the same $\ker C_{j_0}$. But the right null line
of EP.60 is

$$
\left(1,-\frac{b_Kz_0}{h_1x_0},-\frac{d_Kw_0}{h_2x_0}\right)^T,
\tag{EP.62}
$$

which again determines the coefficient ray. Thus all supported live
products of this actual label are proportional to one $K$ and their
coefficient Gram has rank one. All denominators are nonzero. The proof
assumed neither an isometric encoder nor a TP live restriction. Complex
phases, later failure subeffects and arbitrary inaccessible multiplicities
are allowed. Later failure subeffects kill coordinate $0$ and add no
winding moment.

For countable CP branches the same finite-dimensional argument applies.
Under the stipulated integral hypotheses it applies almost everywhere;
if an internal index is unacquired and aggregated into a coarse label,
that finite-dimensional CP encoder has its own Kraus representation
and one actual decoder, so the same argument applies to that CP mass.
Hidden indices never select different decoders. $\square$

